---
import LegalPage from '../layouts/LegalPage.astro';

/**
 * DRAFT. Written from scratch in plain language for a Berlin-based workshop
 * marketplace. Every <span class="fill-in"> is a value only the operator
 * knows. Have a German lawyer review before publishing — the platform /
 * intermediary construction in section 2 and the withdrawal-right carve-out
 * in section 12 are the two clauses that most need checking against how
 * Twiggli actually contracts and charges.
 */
const toc = [
  { id: 'about', label: '1. Who we are' },
  { id: 'what-twiggli-is', label: '2. What Twiggli is' },
  { id: 'account', label: '3. Your account' },
  { id: 'booking', label: '4. Booking a workshop' },
  { id: 'payment', label: '5. Prices and payment' },
  { id: 'cancellation', label: '6. Cancellations and refunds' },
  { id: 'hosting', label: '7. If you host a workshop' },
  { id: 'content', label: '8. Videos and content you post' },
  { id: 'conduct', label: '9. What you may not do' },
  { id: 'availability', label: '10. Availability of the service' },
  { id: 'liability', label: '11. Our liability' },
  { id: 'withdrawal', label: '12. Right of withdrawal' },
  { id: 'termination', label: '13. Suspension and termination' },
  { id: 'changes', label: '14. Changes to these terms' },
  { id: 'law', label: '15. Governing law and disputes' },
];
---

<LegalPage
  title="Terms of Service — Twiggli"
  kicker="Legal"
  heading="Terms of Service"
  description="The rules for using Twiggli, for both participants and hosts."
  lede="These terms cover using the Twiggli app and website — booking a workshop, and running one. Please read section 2: Twiggli connects you with hosts, but the workshop itself is a contract between you and the host."
  toc={toc}
>
  <div class="legal-notice" slot="notice">
    <p><strong>Draft — not yet reviewed by a lawyer.</strong></p>
    <p>
      This document was written as a clear, plain-language starting point, not
      as legal advice. Highlighted fields still need real values, and a German
      lawyer should review it before it goes live — particularly sections 2, 5,
      6, 11 and 12.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="about">1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Twiggli is operated by <span class="fill-in">[legal company name]</span>,
    <span class="fill-in">[address]</span>, Germany. You can reach us at
    <span class="fill-in">[email address]</span>. Full company details are in
    our <a href="/impressum/">Impressum</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    In these terms, "Twiggli", "we" and "us" mean that company. "You" means
    anyone using our app or website — whether you are booking a workshop or
    running one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-twiggli-is">2. What Twiggli is</h2>
  <p>
    Twiggli is a marketplace. Hosts publish workshops and activities; participants
    find and book them. We provide the platform, the video feed, the booking flow
    and the payment handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>We do not run the workshops.</strong> When you book, the contract for
    that workshop is between you and the host. The host decides what happens, where
    it happens, who is qualified to run it and what it costs. We are not a party to
    that contract, and we are not the host's employer, agent or partner.
  </p>
  <p>
    We check hosts before they can publish, but we cannot guarantee the quality,
    safety or legality of any individual workshop. Use your judgement, and tell us
    if something goes wrong.
  </p>

  <h2 id="account">3. Your account</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You need an account to book or to host. You must be at least 18, or have
      a parent or guardian's consent and involvement.</li>
    <li>Give accurate details and keep them current. Bookings and refunds depend
      on us being able to reach you.</li>
    <li>Keep your login credentials to yourself. Tell us promptly if you think
      someone else has access to your account.</li>
    <li>One account per person. Don't create an account for someone else without
      their knowledge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="booking">4. Booking a workshop</h2>
  <p>
    A listing is an invitation to book, not a binding offer. Your booking becomes
    binding when we confirm it — by email, in the app, or both. Until then, the
    host may still decline or the spot may go to someone else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check the listing before you book: the date, the meeting point, what is
    included, what you need to bring, and any age, fitness or equipment
    requirements. If the host has set participation conditions, they apply to
    your booking.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn up on time. Hosts are not obliged to hold a place for a late arrival, and
    a missed workshop is not automatically refundable — see section 6.
  </p>

  <h2 id="payment">5. Prices and payment</h2>
  <p>
    Prices are shown in euros and include VAT where it applies. The price you see
    at checkout is the price you pay.
  </p>
  <p>
    Payments are processed by <span class="fill-in">[payment provider]</span>. We
    do not store your full card details. Your payment provider's terms apply
    alongside ours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Listing a workshop is free. When a paid booking completes, we deduct a service
    fee of <span class="fill-in">[fee %]</span> from the host's payout. Hosts are
    paid out <span class="fill-in">[payout schedule, e.g. within 7 days of the
    workshop taking place]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free workshops carry no fee for anyone. A host may still ask participants to
    cover materials directly, but that must be stated clearly in the listing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancellation">6. Cancellations and refunds</h2>
  <h3>If you cancel</h3>
  <p>
    You can cancel a booking in the app. What you get back depends on when you
    cancel:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>More than <span class="fill-in">[X]</span> hours before the start: full
      refund.</li>
    <li>Less than <span class="fill-in">[X]</span> hours before the start:
      <span class="fill-in">[partial refund / no refund]</span>.</li>
    <li>No-show: no refund.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>If the host cancels</h3>
  <p>
    You get a full refund, automatically, to your original payment method. If a
    host cancels repeatedly or at short notice, we may remove their listings.
  </p>
  <h3>If the workshop changes</h3>
  <p>
    If a host materially changes a workshop after you book — the date, the
    location, or what the session actually is — you may cancel for a full refund.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hosting">7. If you host a workshop</h2>
  <p>When you publish a workshop, you confirm that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You are legally allowed to run it, and you hold any licence, permit or
      qualification it requires.</li>
    <li>Your listing is accurate — including the price, the duration, the
      location, the group size and what participants need to bring.</li>
    <li>You have the right to use every video and photo you upload.</li>
    <li>You have appropriate insurance for the activity, and you are responsible
      for the safety of your session.</li>
    <li>You handle your own taxes. We are not your employer, and hosting income is
      yours to declare. If you host as a business, you must say so — consumer
      protection rules will then apply to your participants.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Show up to your own workshops. If you must cancel, do it as early as you can
    and tell your participants directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="content">8. Videos and content you post</h2>
  <p>
    Your content stays yours. By posting it on Twiggli you give us a
    non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free licence to host, display, resize and
    distribute it for the purpose of running and promoting the platform. That
    licence ends when you delete the content, except where we must keep copies for
    legal reasons or in ordinary backups.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only post what you have the right to post. If you film participants, get their
    agreement first. We remove content that infringes someone else's rights —
    write to <span class="fill-in">[email address]</span> to report something.
  </p>

  <h2 id="conduct">9. What you may not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Break the law, or use Twiggli to arrange something illegal or unsafe.</li>
    <li>Post content that is misleading, hateful, harassing, sexually explicit or
      violent.</li>
    <li>Take bookings or payments off-platform to avoid our fees.</li>
    <li>Post fake listings, fake reviews, or impersonate someone else.</li>
    <li>Scrape, reverse-engineer, overload or attempt to break our systems.</li>
    <li>Use anyone's personal data from a booking for anything other than running
      that workshop.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="availability">10. Availability of the service</h2>
  <p>
    We aim to keep Twiggli running, but we don't promise it will be available
    without interruption. We may change features, pause the service for
    maintenance, or discontinue parts of it. Where a change is significant and
    affects you, we will give reasonable notice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="liability">11. Our liability</h2>
  <p>
    We are fully liable for damage caused intentionally or through gross
    negligence, for injury to life, body or health, and under the German Product
    Liability Act.
  </p>
  <p>
    For ordinary negligence, we are liable only where we breach an obligation that
    is essential to the contract and that you can reasonably rely on us to meet —
    and then only for foreseeable, typical damage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond that, we are not liable. In particular, we are not liable for what
    happens at a workshop, since we do not run it — that is between you and the
    host. Nothing here limits rights you have under mandatory consumer law.
  </p>

  <h2 id="withdrawal">12. Right of withdrawal</h2>
  <p>
    Consumers in the EU normally have 14 days to withdraw from a contract made
    online. That right does <strong>not</strong> apply to leisure services booked
    for a specific date or period — which covers most workshops on Twiggli
    (§ 312g(2) no. 9 of the German Civil Code).
  </p>
  <p>
    Our cancellation policy in section 6 applies instead, and it is often more
    generous. Where the withdrawal right does apply to something you buy from us,
    we will tell you at checkout and give you the standard withdrawal instructions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="termination">13. Suspension and termination</h2>
  <p>
    You can close your account at any time. Bookings already made still stand —
    honour them or cancel them properly first.
  </p>
  <p>
    We may suspend or close an account that breaks these terms, that puts other
    users at risk, or that we are legally required to act on. Where it is
    reasonable to do so, we will tell you why and give you a chance to respond.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">14. Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    We may update these terms. If a change materially affects you, we will notify
    you at least <span class="fill-in">[30]</span> days beforehand by email or in
    the app. If you don't accept the change, you can close your account before it
    takes effect. Continuing to use Twiggli after that date means you accept the
    new terms.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">15. Governing law and disputes</h2>
  <p>
    German law applies. If you are a consumer, this does not deprive you of the
    protection of mandatory law in your country of residence.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are not obliged to take part in dispute resolution proceedings before a
    consumer arbitration board, and we do not currently do so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Questions about these terms: <span class="fill-in">[email address]</span>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
